{
 "id": "l08",
 "label": "8",
 "title": "Words that show possession",
 "summary": "Ni and ng, the possessive pronouns (ákin, ko…), and nitó, niyán, niyón",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Use ni, niná, ng and ng mga to show an owner",
  "Use the possessive pronouns before a noun (áking) and after a noun (ko)",
  "Avoid using the same form twice in one sentence",
  "Place possessives correctly with plural nouns",
  "Use nitó, niyán and niyón"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Ni and ng: of",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To show ownership, **ni** is placed before a **proper noun** (a name such as Maria or Pedro), and **ng** is placed before a **common noun** (such as gurò, teacher, or babáe, woman). Both mean **of**."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In Tagalog, the thing that is possessed must **always come before the owner**. English often does the opposite (*Maria's house*), so the order feels backwards at first."
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Thing first, owner second",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Name",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "ang",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "báhay",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ni",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "Maria",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Common noun",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "ang",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "báhay",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ng",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "laláki",
         "r": "noun"
