  {
   "title": "Two possessives in one sentence",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "When **two possessive pronouns** are used in a sentence, a **combination of the two styles** (one before a noun and one after a noun) makes a better sentence than using only one style for both."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Take *Her mother went to her house*, which uses *her* twice:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Sentence",
      "Verdict"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Ang kanyáng iná ay pumuntá sa kanyáng báhay.",
       "Not good: kanyáng is repeated"
      ],
      [
       "Ang iná niyá ay pumuntá sa báhay niyá.",
       "Not good: niyá is repeated"
      ],
      [
       "Ang iná niyá ay pumuntá sa kanyáng báhay.",
       "Good, but iná niyá is a tongue twister"
      ],
      [
       "Ang kanyáng iná ay pumuntá sa báhay niyá.",
       "Good, and it sounds better"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Mixing the two styles"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Why the last one sounds best",
     "t": "It pairs **iná** with **kanyá** and **báhay** with **niyá**, so the tongue-twisting phrase *iná niyá* disappears. Both good sentences are correct. Tagalog speakers prefer not to use the same form of a possessive pronoun twice in one sentence."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "With plural nouns"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "With plural nouns, the possessive pronoun goes **between ang and mga**, and the adjectives should be in their plural forms as well."
    },
