  {
   "title": "Nitó, niyán and niyón",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Itó, iyán and iyón show where an object is in relation to the speaker. As possessives they change into **nitó** (*of this person*) and **niyán** and **niyón** (*of that person*). They point to something that belongs to a person near or far from the speaker or the person spoken to. When you use them, you assume the listener also recognises the owner."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Word",
      "Means",
      "The owner is…"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[nitó|poss]]",
       "of this person",
       "near the speaker"
      ],
      [
       "[[niyán|poss]]",
       "of that person",
       "near the person spoken to"
      ],
      [
       "[[niyón|poss]]",
       "of that person",
       "far from the persons talking"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Possessive this and that"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Always after the noun",
     "t": "Unlike the possessive personal pronouns, nitó, niyán and niyón always stay in the same place **after the noun**, even in an inverted sentence."
    },
