 "recap": [
  "Possession puts the thing possessed first and the owner second: ang báhay ni Maria.",
  "Ni (proper noun), niná (plural), ng (common noun), ng mga (plural). Both ni and ng mean of.",
  "Possessive pronouns before a noun take a ligature (áking, iyóng, kaniyáng, áming, áting, inyóng, kaniláng).",
  "After a noun they change form: ko, mo, niyá, námin, nátin, ninyó, nilá.",
  "Mix the two styles when two possessives are in one sentence. Plural nouns: ang áking mga anák.",
  "Nitó, niyán, niyón (of this/that person) always follow the noun. Plural owners: ng mga itó / iyán / iyón."
 ]
